WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.840
A useful playbook has a predictable anatomy. The exact template can vary,

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:09.220
but the core sections should be consistent enough that responders know where to look under

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:12.240
pressure. Consistency is not bureaucracy.

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:17.340
It is a way to make different incident scenarios easier to compare, test, and maintain.

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:19.880
The first section is purpose and scope.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.700
It explains what scenario the playbook covers and what it does not cover.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:27.840
For example, a phishing playbook might cover reported suspicious email,

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:31.820
confirmed credential harvesting attempts, and malicious attachments,

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:35.000
but it may hand off endpoint infection to a malware playbook.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:39.300
Clear scope prevents teams from forcing every event into the wrong workflow.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.680
Triggering conditions explain when to start.

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:46.460
Triggers can include an alert, a user report, an external notification,

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:51.560
a suspicious login pattern, a malware detection, a data loss prevention event,

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:55.060
an exposed cloud key, or a business owner escalation.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:58.880
A trigger does not prove an incident. It starts triage.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:03.100
Severity guidance then helps responders classify impact and urgency.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:07.800
Severity should consider affected users, systems, data, business services,

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:12.660
regulatory concerns, active attacker behavior, and containment urgency.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:15.600
Roles and responsibilities are the next anchor.

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:19.500
The playbook should identify who triages, who approves containment,

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:23.800
who performs technical actions, who handles legal or privacy review,

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:27.540
who updates stakeholders, and who owns recovery decisions.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:30.640
Required tools and data sources should be listed nearby.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:35.580
These may include endpoint telemetry, authentication logs, cloud audit logs,

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:41.640
email security data, network logs, ticketing systems, asset inventory, backup systems,

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:46.420
or case management records. The response actions are the body of the playbook.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:50.080
They should be written as steps that a trained responder can follow,

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:53.500
but they should not pretend every branch is automatic.

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:58.200
Some steps are required, such as opening a case, preserving key evidence,

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:03.700
recording timestamps, and identifying affected assets. Some steps are optional branches,

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:08.880
such as collecting a memory image only when the host is high value and the organization

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:12.780
has that capability. Some steps are judgment-based decisions,

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:17.480
such as whether to disable an account immediately or preserve access briefly to understand

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:20.260
scope. Decision points should be visible.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:23.440
A decision point might ask whether the incident is active,

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:27.900
whether sensitive data is involved, whether containment will disrupt a critical service,

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:32.520
whether legal review is required, or whether escalation to executives is needed.

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:37.140
Evidence handling notes should tell responders what to collect, where to store it,

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:41.440
and how to document chain of custody when formal evidence handling is required.

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:46.460
Finally, the playbook needs communication paths, containment and recovery options,

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:49.560
closure criteria, and post-incident tasks.

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:54.980
Closure should require more than "the alert stopped." It should confirm that the root

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.360
cause or enabling condition was addressed,

00:02:57.580 --> 00:03:02.140
affected systems recovered, stakeholders updated, documentation completed,

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:04.280
and improvement actions assigned.

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:08.580
A strong playbook guides the incident from first signal to lessons learned.
